{"tool": "perry-detect-host", "args": ["-h"], "exit": 0, "stdout": "perry-detect-host \u2014 print the host runtime so SKILL.md branches can\nchoose the right tool (AskUserQuestion vs free-text, Agent vs codex\nexec, etc.). Output is a single lowercase token to stdout:\n\n  claude-code   running inside Claude Code (CLI / Desktop)\n  codex-cli     running inside OpenAI Codex CLI\n  opencode      running inside OpenCode\n  unknown       cannot determine (treat as claude-code by default in\n                SKILL.md branches; manual override below recommended)\n\nManual override (always wins):\n  export PERRY_HOST=claude-code      # or codex-cli or opencode\n\nDetection is best-effort: env-var sniffing first, parent-process name\nsecond. Both can change as host runtimes evolve; the env override is\nthe durable contract. Valid invocations exit 0; invalid arguments exit 2.\n\nUsage: perry-detect-host [--help]\n", "stderr": ""}
{"tool": "perry-explain", "args": ["V4"], "exit": 0, "stdout": "V4 \u2014 verification rung\n\n  rubric review - a fresh-context reviewer scores against written acceptance criteria. Attested by a second agent.\n\n  defined in schema/state-schema.json \u00a7 verification.rungs\n\n  DESIGN-003 section 5.3. Replaces the binary evidence gate with a declared rung per task. Advisory for one release (decision 4), then a hard gate. Two rules govern it, and neither is expressible as a column check: (1) the required rung is a function of CONSEQUENCE, not shape - anything outward-facing, irreversible, or carrying money/legal/safety exposure requires V5 minimum in every mode, overriding the mode default; (2) a V4 claim must cite an acceptance-criteria file and the reviewer must not have seen the reasoning that produced the artifact, or it is V1 wearing a costume.\n", "stderr": ""}
